Given the reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery (i.e., more probability on unfavorable outcomes), and if we define unfavorable as any loss, then Lottery B has higher probability of loss (25% vs 20%) and thus more probability on unfavorable outcomes. So Lottery B would be considered more right-skewed under that interpretation. Alternatively, if 'unfavorable' refers to the worst possible outcome, then Lottery A has a more unfavorable outcome. But the reason refers to 'a greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable as opposed to favorable outcomes' — plural — suggesting overall probability mass on loss vs no loss. Favorable outcome is 0, unfavorable is any loss. Then Lottery B has 25% on unfavorable, A has 20% — so B is more right-skewed. Therefore, the reason would prefer Lottery B. The reason can be applied to this decision problem, and under this interpretation, it prefers Lottery B.",
