  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing the skewness of the lottery distributions — specifically, how much probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes — and prefers the more right-skewed lottery. As analyzed in Step 1, this would favor Lottery B if 'unfavorable' means any loss (since B has 25% loss probability vs A’s 20%). However, the individual does not consider the overall probability of loss or the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. Instead, they focus solely on the size of the potential loss, choosing the option with the smaller loss amount. This reflects a different decision rule — minimizing the worst possible loss — rather than evaluating the skewness of the distribution. There is no evidence in the verbal report that the individual assessed how much probability was assigned to unfavorable outcomes or compared the shape of the distributions. Therefore, while both the reason and the individual may end up favoring Lottery B, the underlying justification is fundamentally different. The individual did not use skewness-based reasoning. The information considered (loss size) does not match the information required for the decision reason (probability mass on unfavorable outcomes). The justification (smaller loss) does not align with the reason’s rule (prefer more right-skewed). Hence, it is certain that the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
