  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, particularly preferring more right-skewed lotteries (those with higher probability on unfavorable outcomes). However, in this case, Lottery B is not more right-skewed—it has a 50% chance of a favorable outcome (1000) and 50% of an unfavorable (0), while Lottery A is certain at the midpoint. Thus, Lottery B is actually left-skewed in outcome space if 0 is considered unfavorable. The individual did not consider skewness or distributional asymmetry. Instead, they justified their choice based on the higher upside and the perception that 0 is not a loss but neutral, enabling greater risk-taking. There is no alignment between the information used in the decision reason (skewness) and the information considered by the individual (potential gain, risk, framing of zero). The justification also differs: the reason prefers right-skew due to probability concentration on bad outcomes, while the individual embraces risk for higher reward. Therefore, it is highly unlikely the individual used the skewness-based reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
